.TH TSS2-POLICY-CALLBACKS 7 "NOVEMBER 2026" "TPM2 Software Stack"
.SH NAME
tss2-policy-callbacks \- application callbacks for policy calculation and execution
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <tss2/tss2_policy.h>

TSS2_RC Tss2_PolicySetCalcCallbacks(
    TSS2_POLICY_CTX *policy_ctx,
    TSS2_POLICY_CALC_CALLBACKS *calc_callbacks);

TSS2_RC Tss2_PolicySetExecCallbacks(
    TSS2_POLICY_CTX *policy_ctx,
    TSS2_POLICY_EXEC_CALLBACKS *exec_callbacks);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Policy library calculates policy digests and executes TPM policy commands.
Callbacks supply application-specific information and operations, such as
resolving object paths, selecting a branch, obtaining a signature or executing
an authorized subpolicy. The library does not require FAPI; an application may
implement these operations using its own object store and authorization logic.
.PP
Create a context with
.BR Tss2_PolicyInit (),
then register the callbacks required by the policy. Initialize callback
structures to zero before assigning individual fields. Each callback has its
own corresponding
.B *_userdata
field, which is passed unchanged to that callback.
.PP
Both registration functions copy the callback structure. The structure itself
may therefore be a local variable. The data referenced by userdata is not
copied and must remain valid for all calls using it. Passing NULL instead of
a callback structure clears that group of callbacks.
Neither callback group can be changed while calculation or execution is pending.
The hash algorithm and policy authorizations must also remain unchanged until
the operation succeeds or returns a terminal error.
.SH CALCULATION CALLBACKS
Callbacks in
.B TSS2_POLICY_CALC_CALLBACKS
resolve information missing from the JSON policy. A callback is not necessarily
invoked when the policy already contains the required data or a cached digest.
Output structures are supplied by the library; fill them before returning
.BR TSS2_RC_SUCCESS .
.PP
Paths passed to these callbacks are the paths specified in the JSON policy.
Their interpretation and resolution are application-defined; they need not be
filesystem paths or FAPI keystore paths.
.TP
.B cbname / cbname_userdata
Resolve the object path specified in the policy to its TPM object name and
write the name to the supplied
.BR TPM2B_NAME .
.TP
.B cbpublic / cbpublic_userdata
Resolve the key path specified in the policy to its TPM public area and
write the public area to the supplied
.BR TPMT_PUBLIC .
.TP
.B cbnvpublic / cbnvpublic_userdata
Resolve the NV path or NV index specified in the policy to its NV public area
and write the public area to the supplied
.BR TPMS_NV_PUBLIC .
An NV index of zero means that no explicit index was supplied by the policy.
.TP
.B cbpcr / cbpcr_userdata
Resolve the requested PCR selection into a
.B TPML_PCR_SELECTION
and a
.B TPML_DIGEST
containing the corresponding PCR values. Inspect the selector's
.B type
before reading the appropriate member of its
.B selections
union. Fill both output structures consistently.
.SH EXECUTION CALLBACKS
Callbacks in
.B TSS2_POLICY_EXEC_CALLBACKS
provide the following operations. The exact callback typedefs and parameter
lists are declared in
.BR tss2_policy.h .
.TP
.B cbauth / cbauth_userdata
Resolve the object identified by a TPM name and provide its ESYS object handle,
the authorization entity handle and the session used to authorize that entity.
Install any required authorization value in the ESYS context before returning.
These handles must be valid in the ESYS context used for execution.
.TP
.B cbload / cbload_userdata
The public callback type describes loading an object identified by its name and
returning its ESYS handle. The current Policy executor does not invoke this
callback. Do not rely on it to load objects during execution.
.TP
.B cbpolsel / cbpolsel_userdata
Select a PolicyOR branch using the policy description, branch names and branch
count. Write a zero-based index smaller than
.B branch_count
to
.BR branch_idx .
This callback runs during
.BR Tss2_PolicyExecute_Async (),
including for nested branch selections. It does not receive an FAPI authorization
object; pass application context through userdata when needed.
.TP
.B cbsign / cbsign_userdata
Sign the supplied buffer for PolicySigned using the supplied PEM public key,
key hint and hash algorithm. Return the signature bytes and length through the
output parameters, in the representation expected by the TPM signature
conversion: DER encoding for ECDSA, or the raw RSA signature.
The library consumes these bytes after the callback returns and does not free
them. Keep them in application-owned storage, such as userdata, until the
calling Policy API function returns. Returning a pointer to a local array is
invalid.
.TP
.B cbauthpol / cbauthpol_userdata
Locate and execute an authorized policy matching the supplied public key,
hash algorithm and policy reference. Supply the approved policy digest and
signature through the output parameters. The library then verifies the
signature and issues PolicyAuthorize. Use userdata to access the active session
and application policy store. Use a separate Policy context for a subpolicy;
do not restart the context whose callback is currently running.
.TP
.B cbauthnv / cbauthnv_userdata
Locate and execute the policy identified by the supplied NV public area and
hash algorithm before the library issues PolicyAuthorizeNV. The active session
and application state can be supplied through userdata.
.TP
.B cbdup / cbdup_userdata
Provide the object name used by PolicyDuplicationSelect in the supplied
.BR TPM2B_NAME .
.TP
.B cbaction / cbaction_userdata
Perform the application action named by a PolicyAction element.
The action string is supplied by the policy.
.TP
.B cbflushauth / cbflushauth_userdata
Optional ownership hook used by PolicySecret after its authorization callback.
Return true if the engine should flush the transient authorization handle;
return false if the application retains ownership. This callback returns
.BR bool ,
not a response code, and cannot request a retry.
.TP
.B cbcphash / cbcphash_userdata
Optional notification called after successful PolicyCpHash execution.
This callback returns void and cannot report an error or request a retry.
.SH MEMORY AND RESOURCE OWNERSHIP
Input strings, branch-name arrays, buffers and policy structures passed to
callbacks are borrowed. Do not free them or retain their addresses after the
callback; copy any data needed by an asynchronous operation into userdata.
Some callback input parameters lack const qualifiers. Treat these inputs as
read-only; the absence of const does not transfer ownership. Write only the
documented output parameters. Userdata remains application-owned state and
may be updated by the callback.
.PP
Callbacks returning names, public areas, PCR values or TPM signatures fill
caller-supplied structures without transferring an allocated object. The
.B cbsign
signature buffer is the exception described above: it remains application-owned.
.PP
The application creates the TPM policy session, manages application I/O and
cleans up its resources. Keep the same ESYS context and session across
.BR Tss2_PolicyExecute_Finish ()
retries.
.BR Tss2_PolicyFinalize ()
frees the Policy context but does not flush the caller-owned policy session.
.SH ASYNCHRONOUS OPERATION
Most callbacks return
.BR TSS2_RC .
Return
.B TSS2_RC_SUCCESS
when their outputs are ready. To suspend an operation, retain progress in
userdata and return
.BR TSS2_POLICY_RC_TRY_AGAIN .
After servicing the pending application I/O, the application retries the
appropriate Policy API call. A callback may be invoked more than once; avoid
repeating completed side effects and supply all required outputs on success.
.PP
For calculation, call
.BR Tss2_PolicyCalculate_Async ()
once and repeat
.BR Tss2_PolicyCalculate_Finish ()
until it succeeds or returns a terminal error.
For execution, calculate the policy first, then call
.BR Tss2_PolicyExecute_Async ().
If branch selection requests a retry, service the pending work and retry
.BR Tss2_PolicyExecute_Async ();
preparation may revisit earlier selections. After preparation succeeds, call
.BR Tss2_PolicyExecute_Finish ()
and repeat Finish on retries. Do not call Execute_Async again while execution
is pending. Execute_Async does not require an ESYS context or policy session.
.PP
The synchronous
.BR Tss2_PolicyCalculate ()
and
.BR Tss2_PolicyExecute ()
functions retry internally and cannot service an application event loop between
retries. Use Async/Finish when callbacks depend on such an event loop.
.SH RETURN CODES
Callback failures are propagated without changing their error layer. Library
errors use the Policy layer; TPM, ESYS and other component errors retain their
original layer. Compare complete response codes for terminal errors. For retry
handling, inspect both the base code and the layer; a private callback layer
must not accidentally be interpreted as a retry just because its low bits match.
Returning
.B TSS2_POLICY_RC_TRY_AGAIN
from callbacks avoids this ambiguity.
.PP
A required but missing callback can produce
.B TSS2_POLICY_RC_NULL_CALLBACK
or
.BR TSS2_POLICY_RC_NOT_IMPLEMENTED ,
depending on the policy element. Unused callback fields may remain NULL.
.SH EXAMPLE
The following function registers an application action and executes a policy.
It expects an initialized ESYS context and a caller-owned SHA-256 policy
session. The action callback completes synchronously, so the synchronous API
is appropriate. The caller remains responsible for using or flushing the
session afterwards.
.PP
.nf
#include <stddef.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <tss2/tss2_policy.h>

struct app_state {
    unsigned int approvals;
};

static TSS2_RC
approve(const char *action, void *userdata)
{
    struct app_state *app = userdata;

    if (strcmp(action, "approve") != 0)
        return TSS2_POLICY_RC_BAD_VALUE;
    app->approvals++;
    return TSS2_RC_SUCCESS;
}

TSS2_RC
run_policy(ESYS_CONTEXT *esys, ESYS_TR session)
{
    const char *json =
        "{\e"description\e":\e"approval\e",\e"policy\e":["
        "{\e"type\e":\e"POLICYACTION\e",\e"action\e":\e"approve\e"}]}";
    TSS2_POLICY_CTX *policy = NULL;
    struct app_state app = { 0 };
    TSS2_POLICY_EXEC_CALLBACKS callbacks = { 0 };
    TSS2_RC rc;

    callbacks.cbaction = approve;
    callbacks.cbaction_userdata = &app;

    rc = Tss2_PolicyInit(json, TPM2_ALG_SHA256, &policy);
    if (rc != TSS2_RC_SUCCESS)
        return rc;
    rc = Tss2_PolicySetExecCallbacks(policy, &callbacks);
    if (rc == TSS2_RC_SUCCESS)
        rc = Tss2_PolicyCalculate(policy);
    if (rc == TSS2_RC_SUCCESS)
        rc = Tss2_PolicyExecute(policy, esys, session);
    Tss2_PolicyFinalize(&policy);
    return rc;
}
.fi
.PP
PolicyAction invokes application code; it does not itself enforce a TPM policy
restriction. A real authorization policy should include the TPM policy elements
required by the application.
.SH "LINKING"
Use the compiler and linker flags from
.BR "pkg-config --cflags --libs tss2-policy" .
